WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.120
A female Arthur, a fanatical religious warrior,&nbsp;
and a pious politician who murders men with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:11.940
her bare hands; the women of "The Last Kingdom"&nbsp;
are badass even without fire-breathing dragons.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:18.180
When HBO's "Game of Thrones" debuted in&nbsp;
2011, it rekindled interest in medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:23.100
and medieval-inspired stories in much the same way&nbsp;
"The Lord of the Rings" films reignited interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:29.700
in epic fantasy. Since both relied on fantasy and&nbsp;
European medieval history, it was no surprise when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.680
a crop of new series incorporating those elements&nbsp;
began to appear on the network streaming scene.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:40.560
But, as popular as these new series have proven to&nbsp;
be with modern audiences, they've all failed in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:45.120
very particular way. While seeming to promote&nbsp;
strong female characters, most don't provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.980
the women in their stories with the emotional&nbsp;
complexity of the male characters. There is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.780
however, one medieval drama that rejects&nbsp;
that trend: Netflix's "The Last Kingdom."

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:59.400
While shows based on history or the literary&nbsp;
traditions of the medieval period cannot escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.140
the influence of the early Church, many&nbsp;
don't even try to avoid the trope trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.820
offering only a one-dimensional view of&nbsp;
women. "The Last Kingdom" is an exception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:11.760
evading these pitfalls by&nbsp;
constructing fully-developed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.200
powerful female characters while staying true&nbsp;
to the historical realities of the period.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.960
Because of its dependence on nonfiction sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.300
"The Last Kingdom" might seem even more confined&nbsp;
by the era's restrictions on women than most,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.520
but the series' female characters exhibit&nbsp;
genuine humanity and portray a range of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.960
emotions and strength that assumes women of the&nbsp;
past were just as complex as they are today.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:39.180
Unfortunately, the same approach to female&nbsp;
characters is not taken in other popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.220
medieval or medieval-inspired series. "Game of&nbsp;
Thrones," for instance, fails when it comes to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:49.020
giving its so-called "strong female characters"&nbsp;
the complexity and realism of its male characters.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:54.780
While Tyrion Lannister and Petyr Baelish are cut&nbsp;
from the same cunning cloth, they possess vastly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.160
different motivations and viewpoints. Contrast&nbsp;
this with Sansa Stark, the typical "virgin"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.620
archetype, who then transforms into the equally&nbsp;
stock "political warrior" only after she suffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:09.540
serious abuse at the hands of men. A similar&nbsp;
path is followed by Daenerys Targaryen and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.340
Cersei Lannister as they each descend into their&nbsp;
versions of the role of a standard "evil queen."

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.840
In what seems to be a nod to more progressive&nbsp;
women, the series offers Brienne of Tarth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:24.000
Melisandre, Sadly, this turns out to be just&nbsp;
more of the same in a new pair of archetypes;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:28.260
the Arthurian "Lady of the Lake" Melisandre&nbsp;
and the "Saint Joan-like" Brienne of Tarth.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:32.460
"All my life, men like you have&nbsp;
sneered at me, and all my life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.320
I've been knocking men like you into the dust."

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.360
In fact, except for the archetypes of Brienne&nbsp;
and Melisandre, and possibly Arya Stark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:44.820
an embodiment of the "tomboy/child" archetype,&nbsp;
the women of "Game of Thrones" are defined and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:49.500
driven by the actions of men. The problem with&nbsp;
the series isn't so much that it mistreats its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:53.640
female characters; it simply never allows&nbsp;
them to rise above their tired tropes.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.500
This trend pops up again in "Vikings," with its&nbsp;
stock "vengeance warrior" Lagertha locked in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:03.360
conflict with its stock "seductress/witch"&nbsp;
Queen Aslaug. The sequel series "Vikings:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:07.320
Valhalla" kicked off with a supposedly&nbsp;
strong female lead character, only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.820
reveal that these qualities had developed as&nbsp;
a response to victimization by men. Finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.620
in the short-lived series "Knightfall," Isabella&nbsp;
and Joan are little more than reincarnations of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.580
Queen Guinevere, the "good woman" trope&nbsp;
who's driven to sin by sexual desire.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.400
Although it has roots in early medieval history,&nbsp;
"The Last Kingdom" is far from a docu-drama for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.540
history nerds. Providing some significant&nbsp;
female characters, the women of "The Last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:35.640
Kingdom" are messy, relatable, dynamic people.&nbsp;
The nun, Hild, for example, draws strength from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:41.340
her anger but refuses to let hate disillusion&nbsp;
her. In contrast, Brida, the Viking, is driven&nbsp;

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.180
by a complex combination of faith,&nbsp;
grief, betrayal, and loneliness.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.560
Only in her final confrontation with&nbsp;
her former friend and lover, Uhtred,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:54.060
can Brida finally resolve her inner conflicts
and find the strength to forgive him. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.660
it's authenticity like this that makes&nbsp;
the women in "The Last Kingdom" more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:01.920
relatable and powerful than women who rely on&nbsp;
destroying cities with fire-breathing dragons.

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.860
Brida's character is contrasted with Aethelflaed,&nbsp;
the Lady of Mercia. Based on an actual historical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:12.720
figure, Aethelflaed provides another depiction&nbsp;
of power and strength, far more subtle than an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.720 --> 00:04:17.220
action-figure shield maiden or evil queen. Not&nbsp;
only does she give up the love of her life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.280
but she also faces her own mortality with the&nbsp;
grace and humility of a tragic female Arthur. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:28.200
other series, women may be allowed to make a noble&nbsp;
sacrifice to prove their strength, but typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:33.120
it's made out of love for a man or a child and&nbsp;
not out of her desire to protect her kingdom.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.880
In Stiorra and Eadith, "The Last Kingdom" even&nbsp;
manages to put a fresh twist on the age-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.180
warrior/virgin and sorceress/whore archetypes.&nbsp;
Uhtred's Viking daughter Stiorra loses her husband&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:49.860
due to her father's loyalties, but decides&nbsp;
not to let the unjust actions of men define&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:55.020
her. Though she does take her revenge on Brida,&nbsp;
it's an act she considers necessary to achieve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.940
peace. Refusing to follow Uhtred's orders and come&nbsp;
home, she takes command of her husband's Vikings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:03.960
proving to be a forward-thinking leader&nbsp;
capable of strategic thought. And yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:09.420
when the most unlikely person begs for her help,&nbsp;
Stiorra has the humanity and strength to forgive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:13.500
a decision that has a major impact on&nbsp;
the series' portrayal of English history.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.000
As for Eadith, while initially presented&nbsp;
as a seductress, the character takes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:22.200
different arc. Leaving England to study, she&nbsp;
becomes a doctor and returns to the island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:26.400
not as a romantic interest for&nbsp;
Uhtred but as a sought-after healer.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:30.660
While all the women of "The Last Kingdom''&nbsp;
are complex flesh and blood characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.820
Queen Aelswith is perhaps the best example of&nbsp;
the series' commitment to presenting women as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:41.460
human beings. In the show's first two seasons,&nbsp;
Aelswith's aggressive piety and hatred of Uhtred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.620
sets her up as a series villain. Over time,&nbsp;
however, she draws on surprising reserves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:50.940
of inner strength, becoming the force behind&nbsp;
much of the series' action while maneuvering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.020
around the restrictions of her gender.&nbsp;
What's more, this growth is managed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.860
her own terms and not as a reaction to&nbsp;
the trauma suffered at the hands of men.

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.480
After the death of her daughter, Aethelflaed,&nbsp;
Aelswith's vulnerability allows her to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:08.400
the strength to trust her longtime foe. That&nbsp;
vulnerability, along with the realistic pace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:13.140
of her evolution, transforms Aelswith from a&nbsp;
woman motivated by blind faith and fear into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:17.460
one of the series' strongest characters.&nbsp;
She even develops a wry sense of humor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:20.280
describing how she once killed&nbsp;
a man with just her bare hands:

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:26.100
"I drove a knife through his&nbsp;
neck. I was not wearing gloves."

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.540
Ultimately, what sets "The Last Kingdom"&nbsp;
apart as a series is that its women are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:34.860
not defined solely by the actions of men.&nbsp;
They are not forced to conform to the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:39.720
dreary stereotypes but are portrayed as&nbsp;
real people with complexity and courage.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.720
"It has been an eventful time."

